 "Then merge the small pieces back up to chunk_size, with overlap.": "",
 "Goal: keep semantically related text together. A paragraph boundary is a better cut than the middle of a word.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>Live demo</0>": "",
 "The recursive splitter tries the <0>biggest natural boundary first</0> (paragraphs <1/>), falls back to lines, sentences, then words, only when a piece is still larger than <2/>.": "",
 "Small pieces are <0>merged</0> back up toward <1/>; <2/> repeats the tail of one chunk at the head of the next, so facts on a boundary are not lost.": "",
 "Sizes here are in <0>characters</0> (LangChain's default length function); many setups count tokens instead.": "",
 "This is a JS re-implementation of the idea, not the library code; details (separator handling, whitespace stripping) may differ slightly.": "",
 "Trade-off: small chunks retrieve precisely but lose context; large chunks keep context but dilute the embedding and cost more prompt tokens.": "",
